---
scope: src/tools/init/scaffoldCommands
intent: >
  scaffoldCommands installs the six AIDE pipeline slash commands
  (research, spec, plan, build, qa, fix) into an `aide/` subfolder
  of the host framework's command directory. Each command file is a
  faithful render of a canonical command template that lives under
  docs/commands/aide/, read at runtime through initContent. These
  six scaffolded files are the entire runtime of the AIDE pipeline
  in a host project — every pipeline phase the maintainer documents
  only becomes executable in a host project because this helper put
  the slash command there. The `aide/` subfolder is load-bearing:
  host frameworks derive the slash command namespace from folder
  nesting, so a file installed at `<command-dir>/aide/research.md`
  becomes `/aide:research` in the host.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - Exactly six slash command files are scaffolded under an
      `aide/` subfolder, one per pipeline phase: research, spec,
      plan, build, qa, fix. The count is capped because pipeline-
      phase dilution at the command layer is the same regression as
      tool-surface dilution at the handshake layer. The filenames
      are bare phase names (`research.md`, not `aide-research.md`)
      because the enclosing `aide/` folder already carries the
      namespace — double-naming would produce `/aide:aide-research`
      on the host side.
    - Each command file is a byte-faithful render of its canonical
      template read from docs/commands/aide/ at runtime. A diff
      between an installed command file and its template shows only
      the framework-specific wrapper (a frontmatter block, a header),
      never content paraphrases, reorderings, or summaries.
    - The helper is idempotent per command. An existing command file
      is left untouched and reported as `exists`; only missing files
      are created. A user who has customized any scaffolded command
      keeps their customization verbatim through re-runs.
    - Each command's status — `created` or `exists` — is reported
      independently in the final results, so the init summary can
      show exactly which commands were new on this run and which
      were already in place.
  undesired:
    - Command template content hand-written in this helper's source
      as template literals. Same failure mode as the methodology
      writer: the fix is to read from docs/ through initContent,
      not to centralize the literals in this file.
    - Overwriting an existing command file because "the canonical
      template is authoritative." It is authoritative for new
      installs only. User customizations survive; the re-run reports
      `exists`, not `updated`.
    - A seventh slash command added because a pipeline-phase
      responsibility "felt different" from the existing six.
      Pipeline dilution at the command layer fragments the workflow
      and forces the agent to guess which command to run.
    - Scaffolding into the command directory root instead of an
      `aide/` subfolder, producing flat filenames like
      `aide-research.md` that collide with the host's other commands
      and lose the namespace grouping the canonical docs mandate.
      The folder-as-namespace layout is the only correct shape.
    - A run that fails the whole step if one command template is
      missing. Each of the six commands is independently installable
      and independently reportable; one missing template is one
      failed command, not a cascade failure.
    - Scaffolding commands directly from disk reads of docs/. This
      helper must go through initContent; bypassing it breaks the
      single-reader invariant the init subtree depends on.
---

## Context

The scaffolded slash commands are the only mechanism by which the
maintainer's documented pipeline phases become actual runnable
agent sessions inside a host project. The docs describe the pipeline;
the commands execute it. Without this helper, the pipeline exists
only as documentation, and a developer adopting AIDE would have to
recreate each phase by hand. That is the cold-start gap the parent
spec exists to eliminate, and this helper is one of the two places
(along with writeMethodology) where that elimination actually
happens.

Each command file is small and discrete — one pipeline phase, one
checklist — but together they are load-bearing: delete any one of
them and the pipeline has a hole. The six-count cap is not
cosmetic; it enforces that the pipeline has exactly the phases the
canonical docs describe, and no agent, command, or human can dilute
it by adding a seventh.

## Strategy

**Templates read from docs/commands/aide/ at runtime through
initContent.** The canonical command templates live as Markdown
files under docs/commands/aide/, one per invocable phase. This
helper asks initContent for each template by canonical name,
receives the file's bytes verbatim, applies the framework-specific
wrapper if any, and writes the result into an `aide/` subfolder of
the host's command directory. A template edit in docs/commands/aide/
propagates automatically on the next init run in every host project.

**Install into an `aide/` subfolder, never flat.** Host frameworks
that support slash commands treat nested folders as namespace
prefixes: a file at `<command-dir>/aide/research.md` becomes
`/aide:research`. Installing to the command directory root with a
dash-prefixed filename (`aide-research.md`) is the wrong layout —
it would break the namespace grouping the canonical command-hub
spec requires and double-name the phase at every reference.

**Existing files are immutable.** If the target path already has a
command file, the helper reports `exists` and moves on. It does not
compare content, diff against the template, or offer to upgrade.
The user may have customized the command — for their team's
workflow, for their domain, for debugging. Customizations are
first-class and survive re-runs unconditionally. A future explicit
`aide_upgrade` path can handle intentional re-sync; this helper
never performs one by accident.

**Six commands, counted and named.** The helper iterates over a
fixed list of six canonical command names. Adding a seventh requires
adding a seventh canonical template under docs/commands/aide/,
updating the parent spec to justify the new phase, and changing the
list here. All three changes land in the same PR or none of them
do — this is the three-way lock the canonical command-hub spec
declares mandatory. A helper that discovers its command list
dynamically from the docs folder is a regression, because it lets
a stray Markdown file in the wrong place silently expand the
pipeline.

**Per-command status, not a single aggregate.** Each install reports
its own result. The init summary needs to show "research: created,
spec: exists, plan: created, build: created, qa: created, fix: exists"
so a developer reading the output knows exactly which files are new
on this run. A single aggregate status hides the mix and forces the
developer to inspect the directory manually.

## Good examples

A host project runs `aide_init` for the first time. The scaffold
helper reads six templates from docs/commands/aide/ through
initContent, writes six files into `.claude/commands/aide/` (or
the equivalent for the detected framework), and returns six
`created` statuses. The init summary lists each command by name
with its status. The developer can immediately type `/aide:research`
in their next session and the pipeline begins.

A developer has customized `.claude/commands/aide/build.md` to add
project-specific build steps. They run `aide_init` again to pick up
a new MCP server registration. The scaffold helper sees the
existing build file, reports `exists`, and leaves the customization
intact. The other five commands also report `exists`. The
developer's customization survived, and the re-run was safe.

A maintainer edits the canonical research template in
docs/commands/aide/research.md to clarify the "conflicts resolved"
checklist item. They release a new version of the MCP server. A
fresh host project runs `aide_init`; the new template text lands
in the new project's `aide/research.md` command file on first
install. A host project that was already initialized before the
edit does not automatically pick up the change — its existing file
is preserved — and a future explicit upgrade path is the correct
mechanism for intentional re-sync.

## Bad examples

An installed command file that contains "Search for existing
research: vault notes, transcripts, external sources" when the
current canonical template in docs/ says "Search the brain, then
external sources, then team notes, in that order". The scaffold
helper was composing from a template literal baked into its source
instead of reading from docs/. The host project's pipeline now
teaches a stale workflow; the maintainer has no way to know.

A re-run that overwrites a developer's customized `aide/qa.md`
with the canonical template "because the canonical is authoritative".
The customization — which added a team-specific audit checklist —
is gone, the developer notices only when their next QA session
feels wrong, and trust in the tool is broken.

A scaffold helper that installs flat dash-prefixed files like
`.claude/commands/aide-research.md` into the command directory root
instead of `.claude/commands/aide/research.md`. The host's slash
command becomes `/aide-research` instead of `/aide:research`, the
namespace grouping is gone, and every other scaffolded phase
collides with anything else the host has at the top level of the
command directory. The folder-as-namespace layout was the whole
contract with the canonical command-hub spec, and it was skipped.

A scaffold helper that walks the docs/commands/aide/ directory at
runtime and installs every .md file it finds. A stray scratch file
committed to docs/commands/aide/ accidentally ships as a seventh
slash command. The six-cap invariant was enforced nowhere, the
pipeline dilutes silently, and nobody notices until an agent runs
`/aide:wip` expecting something real.

A scaffold helper that catches a missing template for one command
and aborts the whole step, returning zero installs. The other five
commands could have been written successfully; instead the host
project has a broken init and no pipeline at all, because one
template was misplaced. Independent per-command reporting was the
whole point.
